::CW_U01_L01_Q01::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which sentence best describes the purpose of workshop norms in Creative Writing?{
=A. Workshop norms help writers share, listen, respond respectfully, and revise with clear support.#Correct. Workshop norms protect the writer and make feedback useful for revision.
~B. Workshop norms make every writer use the same style.#Incorrect. Norms guide the process, but writers keep their individual styles.
~C. Workshop norms replace revision because feedback is enough.#Incorrect. Feedback supports revision; it does not replace revision.
~D. Workshop norms are only needed for published authors.#Incorrect. Student writers also need clear routines for sharing and responding.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q02::[MLA.CW.COM.02] A student writes, "The bus doors sighed open, and cold air slid over my shoes." Which feedback is most useful?{
~A. This is cool.#Incorrect. This is too general to help the writer revise.
~B. I do not like buses.#Incorrect. This focuses on the reader's preference instead of the writing.
=C. The personification in "doors sighed open" creates a tired feeling. You might continue that mood with another sound detail.#Correct. The response names craft, explains effect, and gives a revision option.
~D. You should delete the sentence because it has a bus in it.#Incorrect. This gives an unsupported command.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q03::[MLA.CW.COM.04] Which choice uses academic craft language correctly?{
~A. The line is very writing-ish.#Incorrect. This is not precise academic or craft language.
=B. The image of cold air creates a quiet, tense mood.#Correct. This uses craft vocabulary to explain the effect of an image.
~C. The author did stuff with words.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not name a craft choice.
~D. The paragraph has a vibe.#Incorrect. "Vibe" is informal and unexplained here.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q04::[MLA.CW.COM.05] During peer feedback, a writer looks confused after hearing a comment. What should the feedback partner do?{
~A. Repeat the same words louder.#Incorrect. Louder speech does not automatically make feedback clearer.
~B. Move on because confusion is the writer's problem.#Incorrect. Workshop communication should support understanding.
~C. Tell the writer the draft is wrong.#Incorrect. This is disrespectful and unclear.
=D. Restate the comment with a specific example from the draft.#Correct. Restating with evidence supports clear social and instructional communication.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q05::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which feedback comment focuses on the writing rather than judging the writer?{
~A. You are naturally better than everyone else.#Incorrect. This judges the writer, not a craft choice in the writing.
=B. The repeated phrase in the final paragraph creates a strong closing rhythm.#Correct. This focuses on a specific craft choice and its effect.
~C. You are not a story person.#Incorrect. This is a personal judgment and is not useful.
~D. You should write like me.#Incorrect. This removes the writer's control and does not discuss the draft.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q06::[MLA.CW.COM.02] A writer asks for help with setting. Which question would best guide useful feedback?{
~A. Why did you write this at all?#Incorrect. This is not respectful or focused on setting.
~B. Can I change the whole thing for you?#Incorrect. Workshop feedback should not take control from the writer.
=C. Which place details do you want the reader to notice first?#Correct. This question focuses on setting and helps the writer make a craft decision.
~D. Did you finish the quiz yet?#Incorrect. This does not address the writer's request.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q07::[MLA.CW.COM.04] Why are sentence frames useful in a writing workshop?{
~A. They prevent students from having original ideas.#Incorrect. Sentence frames support communication; they do not limit original thinking.
~B. They make every draft sound identical.#Incorrect. Frames guide discussion, not the creative draft itself.
~C. They remove the need to read the draft.#Incorrect. Feedback still must come from the actual writing.
=D. They help students use clear academic language to discuss craft choices.#Correct. Frames give access to precise workshop language.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q08::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which response best shows active listening after a peer reads a draft aloud?{
=A. "I heard the phrase 'empty hallway' twice, and I think that repetition made the scene feel lonely."#Correct. This shows attention to specific language and explains its effect.
~B. "I was thinking about my own story the whole time."#Incorrect. This admits the listener was not attending to the peer's draft.
~C. "I missed most of it, but it was fine."#Incorrect. This does not provide useful feedback.
~D. "You should read faster next time."#Incorrect. This focuses on delivery speed instead of the writing unless delivery was the requested focus.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q09::[MLA.CW.COM.05] Which statement supports respectful workshop culture?{
~A. Only confident writers should share drafts.#Incorrect. Workshop culture should support writers at different readiness levels.
~B. Feedback should embarrass writers so they improve faster.#Incorrect. Embarrassment damages trust and does not support learning.
=C. Writers can ask for feedback on a specific part of the draft.#Correct. This helps feedback match the writer's revision need.
~D. The loudest person should decide which draft is best.#Incorrect. Workshop is not a popularity contest or volume contest.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q10::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which comment gives a specific next step?{
~A. Good job.#Incorrect. This offers no revision direction.
~B. It needs something.#Incorrect. "Something" is too vague to guide revision.
~C. I was confused.#Incorrect. This may be honest but needs a specific location or next step.
=D. Add one sensory detail after the door opens so the setting becomes clearer.#Correct. This gives a focused action the writer can try.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q11::[MLA.CW.COM.02] A peer says, "Your character seems nervous." What addition would make the feedback more useful?{
~A. "because I said so."#Incorrect. This does not provide evidence from the draft.
=B. "because she folds and unfolds the paper before answering."#Correct. This points to a specific action as evidence.
~C. "and everyone knows nervous characters are bad."#Incorrect. This is judgmental and inaccurate.
~D. "but I did not read the paragraph."#Incorrect. Useful feedback must be based on the draft.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q12::[MLA.CW.COM.04] Which phrase best uses craft vocabulary?{
~A. The part near the thing is nice.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. The words are wordy.#Incorrect. This is unclear and not useful.
=C. The dialogue reveals the character's hesitation.#Correct. This uses craft vocabulary and explains function.
~D. The paper is long enough.#Incorrect. Length alone does not discuss craft.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q13::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which workshop behavior should happen before giving feedback?{
~A. Deciding the draft is bad before hearing it.#Incorrect. Feedback should be based on the actual draft.
~B. Interrupting with a personal story.#Incorrect. This takes attention away from the writer.
~C. Choosing a grade for the writer.#Incorrect. Peer workshop is for response and revision, not grading peers.
=D. Listening to the full excerpt and noting specific craft moments.#Correct. Active listening comes before useful feedback.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q14::[MLA.CW.COM.05] Which response is most appropriate if a peer's feedback is unclear?{
~A. "Never mind, your comment does not matter."#Incorrect. This is disrespectful.
=B. "Can you point to the sentence you mean?"#Correct. This asks for clarification in a respectful, instructional way.
~C. "You are wrong."#Incorrect. This shuts down communication.
~D. "I will ignore all feedback."#Incorrect. Writers should consider feedback, even if they do not use every suggestion.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q15::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which feedback best addresses character development?{
~A. The margins look neat.#Incorrect. This is about formatting, not character development.
~B. The font is readable.#Incorrect. This does not address character.
~C. I like characters in general.#Incorrect. This is too general.
=D. The character's choice to hide the letter shows fear, but adding one thought could clarify what she fears.#Correct. This identifies character evidence and suggests a revision.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q16::[MLA.CW.COM.04] A student says, "I don't know how to talk about craft." Which support would help most?{
~A. Avoid all discussion.#Incorrect. Avoidance does not build workshop skill.
=B. Use a frame such as "I noticed ___, and it creates ___."#Correct. The frame supports academic craft language.
~C. Say only "good" or "bad."#Incorrect. Those words are too general.
~D. Let someone else speak for the student forever.#Incorrect. Support should build the student's ability to participate.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q17::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Why should feedback include evidence from the draft?{
=A. Evidence shows the writer exactly which craft choice created the reader's response.#Correct. Specific evidence makes feedback usable.
~B. Evidence makes feedback longer even when it is unclear.#Incorrect. The purpose is clarity, not length.
~C. Evidence proves the peer owns the draft.#Incorrect. The writer owns the draft.
~D. Evidence replaces revision.#Incorrect. Evidence guides revision but does not replace it.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q18::[MLA.CW.COM.05] Which workshop statement respects writer ownership?{
~A. "I rewrote your ending because mine is better."#Incorrect. This takes control from the writer.
~B. "You have to use every suggestion."#Incorrect. Writers consider feedback but make final revision choices.
=C. "One option is to add a final image, but you can decide whether that fits your purpose."#Correct. This offers a choice and respects the writer's purpose.
~D. "Your story should become my story."#Incorrect. This does not respect writer ownership.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q19::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which comment is too vague for a writer to use?{
~A. The dialogue sounds tense because the characters avoid answering each other.#Incorrect. This is specific and useful.
~B. The last image could return to the first image to create a stronger ending.#Incorrect. This gives a focused revision move.
~C. The setting detail about the flickering light creates suspense.#Incorrect. This names a detail and its effect.
=D. Make the whole thing better.#Correct. This is too broad and gives no specific next step.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q20::[MLA.CW.COM.04] Which feedback sentence best supports an English learner's access to the discussion?{
~A. "You know what I mean."#Incorrect. This assumes understanding without clarity.
=B. "By tone, I mean the attitude the scene creates, such as tense or playful."#Correct. This defines academic language during the discussion.
~C. "This is obvious."#Incorrect. This discourages participation.
~D. "Do not ask vocabulary questions."#Incorrect. Vocabulary questions support learning.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q21::[MLA.CW.COM.02] A student gives feedback: "The scene is confusing." Which revision makes the feedback stronger?{
~A. "The scene is confusing and bad."#Incorrect. This adds judgment but not useful detail.
~B. "The scene is confusing to everyone."#Incorrect. This overgeneralizes and gives no evidence.
=C. "I was confused when the speaker changed from I to she; you might clarify the point of view."#Correct. This locates the problem and suggests a craft fix.
~D. "The scene is confusing, so delete it."#Incorrect. This is too extreme and unsupported.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q22::[MLA.CW.COM.05] Which action helps maintain a productive workshop?{
~A. Laughing at a draft when the writer is serious.#Incorrect. This breaks trust and respect.
~B. Sharing another student's draft without permission.#Incorrect. Drafts should be treated respectfully.
~C. Competing to prove whose writing is best.#Incorrect. Workshop supports revision, not competition.
=D. Asking what kind of feedback the writer wants before responding.#Correct. This aligns feedback to the writer's need.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q23::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which feedback best focuses on imagery?{
=A. The smell of smoke and oranges creates a sharp image; you might add where the smell comes from.#Correct. This names imagery, explains effect, and suggests development.
~B. I like stories with smells.#Incorrect. This is a personal preference, not focused feedback.
~C. The story should be about a different topic.#Incorrect. This does not respond to imagery in the draft.
~D. You used words.#Incorrect. This is too vague to be useful.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q24::[MLA.CW.COM.04] What is the best reason to use words like image, diction, dialogue, setting, and tone in feedback?{
~A. They make feedback sound complicated even when it is empty.#Incorrect. Craft vocabulary should clarify, not decorate.
~B. They replace the need for examples.#Incorrect. Craft words still need evidence from the draft.
=C. They help writers discuss specific craft choices clearly.#Correct. Precise terms make feedback more useful.
~D. They prove the peer is always correct.#Incorrect. Vocabulary does not make every interpretation correct.
}
::CW_U01_L01_Q25::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which response best meets Lesson 1 mastery expectations?{
~A. "I liked it."#Incorrect. This is not specific enough for mastery.
~B. "Change everything."#Incorrect. This is not focused or respectful.
~C. "It is fine."#Incorrect. This does not name craft or guide revision.
=D. "The repeated hallway image creates loneliness; consider returning to it in the ending so the draft feels connected."#Correct. This is specific, craft-based, respectful, and revision-focused.
}
